---
import SiteLayout from "../../../../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import Eyebrow from "../../../../../../components/Eyebrow.astro";
---
<SiteLayout title="Module 8: Network Layer — fred.fish" description="Source-based lessons on IP packets, logical addressing, routing tables, and host forwarding decisions." active="cisco">
  <section class="source-lesson">
    <div class="lesson-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/semester-1/">← Semester 1 course</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
    <header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 08" label="MODULE 8 / NETWORK LAYER" /><h1>Address the destination.<br /><span>Choose the next hop.</span></h1><p>The network layer provides logical addressing and moves packets between networks. Hosts and routers use routing information to decide where a packet should go next.</p></header>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Network-layer responsibilities</h2><p>The network layer provides logical addressing and routing between networks. It identifies the source and destination IP addresses, selects a path toward the destination, and forwards packets across interconnected networks. IP is connectionless and best-effort: it does not guarantee delivery, ordering, or recovery from loss. Those services, when needed, are provided by higher-layer protocols.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0641 · IMG-0764</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Understand the IP packet</h2><p>IPv4 and IPv6 packets carry source and destination IP addresses and control information used for forwarding. Routers inspect the destination address and relevant header fields, then choose an outgoing interface or next hop. IPv4 uses a Time to Live (TTL) field to prevent packets circulating indefinitely. IPv6 uses a Hop Limit field for the same purpose. Each router decrements the value before forwarding.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE TOPICS</span><code>IPv4 packet · IPv6 packet</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>How a host decides where to send a packet</h2><p>A host compares the destination IP address with its own subnet. If the destination is local, the host sends directly to that device after resolving its link-layer address. If the destination is remote, the host sends the packet to its configured default gateway. The IP destination remains the final remote host; the first-hop frame is addressed to the gateway's MAC address.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0636 · IMG-0643 · IMG-0195</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">04</div><div><h2>Read a router routing table</h2><p>A router's routing table contains destination prefixes and instructions for reaching them. Directly connected routes are learned from active interfaces. Static routes are configured by an administrator. Dynamic routing protocols exchange route information with other routers. When multiple routes match a destination, the router uses longest-prefix match to choose the most specific route. If no more-specific route matches, a configured default route may be used.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0602 · IMG-0639</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Follow a packet to a remote network.</h2><p>Practice host routing, default gateways, IP addressing, and longest-prefix match.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
